  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nCurrent upper level pattern shows southwest flow with troughing\nover the west coast. Multiple rounds of energy will work their way\nthrough the region as troughing moves south and east.\nPrecipitation amounts continue the downward trend for this evening\nand overnight. Areas along the MT/Dakotas border have about a\n20-40% chance for at least a quarter inch of precipitation, with\nthe Beartooth/Absarokas having similar chances for a half an\ninch Sunday night into Monday. With precipitable water values\ncontinuing to be elevated across SE MT, locally higher rainfall  \namounts are possible with any stronger thunderstorms that form. \nOne limiting factor for thunderstorm development continues to be a\ncap/inversion in model soundings for this area. Hi-res ensembles \nand models continue to show MUCAPE values of 500-1000 J/kg and \n35-45 kts of bulk shear. If storms are able to tap into the energy\navailable, heavy rain, small hail and gusty winds are likely. The\nbest timeframe for storms this evening and overnight looks to be \n9pm-6am MDT Monday morning. There is then about a 20% chance for \nlow stratus/fog to develop Monday morning for SE MT as well. \nAdditional scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \npossible into Monday afternoon mainly across the higher terrain \nand southeast MT. Low clouds may limit thunderstorm development as\ndryer air and clearing does not move in until the evening. Timing\nfor potential thunderstorms Monday looks to be mainly during the \nafternoon hours. Dryer air moves in Tuesday as west to northwest \nflow moves in and persists through the week. High temperatures \nwill remain seasonable in the 60s to low 70s, with Tuesday \ntrending warmer in the upper 70s. TS",
